</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation on synthetic scenario</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S6.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the cost advantages of the proposed approach. <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-P</em> achieves a combined cost that is <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.69\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23.69</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.69\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower than <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS-P</em>, benefiting from the use of deep neutral networks for function approximation. This assists <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-P</em> to observe the environment and strategically select plan groups that reduce discomfort cost over time, while maintaining low inefficiency cost, see Fig. <a href="#S6.F5.sf3" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(c)</span></a>. Similarly, <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-B</em> optimizes behavior ranges for agents by recommending them to behave selfishly at the beginning and then altruistically, minimizing combined costs. However, <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-B</em> tends to coordinate agents toward plans with higher discomfort cost, leading to a combined cost that is <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.81\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.81</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.81\%</annotation></semantics></math> higher than <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-P</em>. By integrating the strengths of both high-level strategies, <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> learns to optimize both plan groups and agent behaviors, achieving the lowest combined cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, with the help of effective coordination through collective learning, <em id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> avoids unnecessary exploration, allowing it to more efficiently converge on the optimized global plan. As a result, it achieves <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.53\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower discomfort cost and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27.05\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>27.05</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27.05\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower inefficiency cost compared to <em id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em>. Note that the low-level policy on plan selection of <em id="S6.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRL</em> heavily depends on its high-level policy on plan constraints. The changing high-level policy leads to the plans selected by low-level policy are changing even taking the same action, which leading to slower training convergence, see Fig. <a href="#S6.F5.sf1" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>, and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27.79\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>27.79</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27.79\%</annotation></semantics></math> higher combined cost than <em id="S6.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, the complexity of all methods is compared. Given the number of nodes per layer in the deep neutral networks <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, the state space <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|</annotation></semantics></math>, and the action space <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|</annotation></semantics></math>, the computational complexity of deep neutral networks is approximately <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(C_{\text{dnn}}(|A|))=O(|A|\cdot W+W^{2}+|S|\cdot W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>dnn</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(C_{\text{dnn}}(|A|))=O(|A|\cdot W+W^{2}+|S|\cdot W)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. The comparison of both computational and communication cost is shown in Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, where <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of iterations in <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS</em>; <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of episodes. The results illustrate that the proposed method lowers computational cost by reducing the action space, especially dealing with a large number of plans <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, outperforming <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRL</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, see Fig. <a href="#S6.F5.sf2" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a>. <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> also has lower communication cost as the number of agents increases, due to its efficient tree communication structure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18088v1/Complex_all.svg" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/575;" width="476" height="575"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>Cost comparison in complex synthetic scenarios: (1) Changing the number of agents from <math id="S6.F6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.F6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>160</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160</annotation></semantics></math> and fixing <math id="S6.F6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> plans per agent, <math id="S6.F6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> time periods and target complexity (first row); (2) Changing the number of plans per agent from <math id="S6.F6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.F6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="112" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>112</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">112</annotation></semantics></math> and fixing <math id="S6.F6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> agents, <math id="S6.F6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> time periods and target complexity (second row); (3) Changing the frequency multiple of cosine waves from <math id="S6.F6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/24</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.F6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math> and fixing <math id="S6.F6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> agents, <math id="S6.F6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> plans per agent, and <math id="S6.F6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> time periods (third row).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then, <em id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> is evaluated using complex synthetic scenario by varying number of agents, number of plans and complexity of target tasks, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ VI-A Evaluation on synthetic scenario ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of agents.</span>
If the number of agents increases from <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math>, there are more agents available to effectively coordinate and reach the target tasks. Therefore, the inefficiency cost of methods that use collective learning (<em id="S6.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-P</em>, <em id="S6.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-B</em> and <em id="S6.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em>) drops significantly by around <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, <em id="S6.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em> has higher inefficiency cost with more agents due to the expanded state space, which hinders training efficiency. Moreover, <em id="S6.SS1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> strategically coordinates agents to choose plans with low discomfort cost, achieving approximately <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.34\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.34</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.34\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower combined cost than <em id="S6.SS1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS-P</em>. The results illustrate that <span id="S6.SS1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high number of agents significantly decreases the inefficiency cost of methods using DCL</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of plans.</span>
If the number of plans per agent increases from <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="112" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>112</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">112</annotation></semantics></math>, each agent has more options to respond to the complex environments. Thus, the combined costs of <em id="S6.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> decreases by <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55.91\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>55.91</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55.91\%</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of plans increases, while still being lower than <em id="S6.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS-P</em> by <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48.75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48.75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48.75\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike <em id="S6.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em>, where agents must choose from a significantly larger action space, leading to increased training complexity, the action space of <em id="S6.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> remains constant, contributing to its lower mean discomfort (<math id="S6.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45.94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45.94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45.94\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and inefficiency costs (<math id="S6.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64.26\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64.26</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64.26\%</annotation></semantics></math>). The results illustrate that <span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high number of plans decreases the discomfort and inefficiency cost of methods using DCL</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity of target tasks.</span>
If the frequency multiplier increases from <math id="S6.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/24</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math> (see Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ V Experimental Methodology ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), agents struggle to achieve the collective goal. This leads to a linear increase of both mean discomfort and inefficiency cost in <em id="S6.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS-P</em>. In contrast, <em id="S6.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em> keeps the inefficiency cost relatively low and constant since it learns to effectively explore and select plans close to the cosine waveform with high frequency. Via reinforcement learning, the proposed <em id="S6.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> achieves a combined cost that is <math id="S6.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.44\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>33.44</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.44\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower than <em id="S6.SS1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS-P</em>. However, the combined cost of <em id="S6.SS1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> exceeds <em id="S6.SS1.p7.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-P</em> at the complexity of <math id="S6.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/6</annotation></semantics></math> as its exploration is restricted by the large action space. The results illustrate that <span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high complexity of target tasks increases the discomfort and inefficiency cost of all methods, but more slightly for those using MARL</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation on energy self-management</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The energy application scenario uses a dataset derived by energy disaggregation of the simulated zonal power transmission in the Pacific Northwest Smart Grid Demonstrations Project <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. It contains consumers (agents) who participate equipped with one or more controllable household appliances, e.g., refrigerators, water heaters, heating/cooling systems, and so on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. The energy demand (kW) of consumers, which is recorded every <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5min</annotation></semantics></math> in a <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12h</annotation></semantics></math> span of a day, is modeled as a plan. The plans of each consumer represent different energy consumption patterns generated using a load-shifting strategy for managing electricity use across peak and off-peak times, prompting for grid load balance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Specifically, the measured demand is the first plan; the other <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> plans are generated by shifting the positions of values in the first plan, i.e., shifting the measured demand <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="720" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>720</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">720</annotation></semantics></math> minutes. The discomfort cost of each plan is the amount of minutes shifted compared to the original demand.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this scenario, each consumer selects a plan of energy demand for <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12h</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., a time period from 10:00 to 22:00 in a day) and aggregate the plans selected by others. The total energy demand is obtained by adding up the selected plans element-wise per time period. The goal of system is to mitigate power peaks and reduce the risk of blackouts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> by minimizing the variance of the total energy demand at each time (no target). For example, if consumers notice that energy demand is higher in the morning before <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12:00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>00</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12:00</annotation></semantics></math> compared to other times of the day, they may learn to select a plan with lower energy demand during the morning hours on the following day. We use different plans from the dataset to make the task environment dynamic. Meanwhile, consumers aim to secure their energy needs on peak times and avoid disrupted (or shifted) plans, often prioritizing comfort at the expense of system-wide efficiency and balance. Thus, their objective is to decrease their amount of minutes shifted by choosing a plan with lower discomfort.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S6.F7.sf1" title="In Fig. 7 ‣ VI-B Evaluation on energy self-management ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> shows that the proposed methods outperform <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em> in terms of inefficiency cost, achieving approximately <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36.03\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>36.03</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36.03\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower combined cost, particularly due to the efficient coordination through multi-agent collective learning. Several other metrics are measured, including (a) the mean minutes shifted per agent, and (b) max and min power peak over all periods. Among all methods, <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-B</em> achieves the lowest discomfort and inefficient costs. Compared to <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-P</em>, <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-B</em> helps agents to stay closer to their original energy demands, reducing total deviation time by over <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.8</annotation></semantics></math>k minutes across all time periods, significantly enhancing consumer satisfaction. Furthermore, compared to <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS-P</em>, <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-B</em> reduces max power peaks by <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13.82</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13.82</annotation></semantics></math>kW and enhances min power peaks by <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15.29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15.29</annotation></semantics></math>kW, see Fig. <a href="#S6.F7.sf2" title="In Fig. 7 ‣ VI-B Evaluation on energy self-management ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, thereby improving the power grid stability. In this scenario, the <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouping behavior ranges</em> strategy outperforms <em id="S6.SS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouping plan constraints</em>. These findings suggest that, in environments with a limited number of plans per agent and where Pareto efficiency is the primary goal, it is more effective to group behavior ranges rather than plans.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18088v1/EnergyBasic.svg" id="S6.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/148;" width="224" height="148"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.sf1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The cost comparison.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18088v1/EnergyResponse.svg" id="S6.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:219/155;" width="219" height="155"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.sf2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Total energy demands per time.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Performance comparison of all methods in the energy self-management scenario (<math id="S6.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>160</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160</annotation></semantics></math> consumers, <math id="S6.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> plans per consumer and <math id="S6.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> time periods, i.e., <math id="S6.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> days). Several other metrics are measured, including (a) the total minutes shifted per agent, and (b) max and min power peak over all periods.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation on drone swarm sensing</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This dataset features a fleet of drones that can capture images or videos of vehicle traffic information on public roadways across <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="144" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>144</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">144</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly distributed areas of interest (sensing cells) within a city map <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. The scenario involves a real-world map of Munich, German, imported to the simulations of urban mobility (SUMO)<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
            https://www.eclipse.org/sumo/</span></span></span> to accurately generate realistic flows of around <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,000</annotation></semantics></math> vehicles. The drones aim to collect the required sensing data (target), which allows drones to observe vehicles in each cell. As a consequence, drones accurately detect the early signs of traffic congestion and report to traffic operators to take mitigation actions. Furthermore, the sensing tasks prioritize energy efficiency, aiming to minimize the total energy consumption of drones. Thus, the <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mean discomfort cost</span> in this scenario represents the energy consumption of drones, whereas <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inefficiency cost</span> represents the sensing inaccuracy that measures the mismatch between overall sensing of drones and sensing requirements (target). The requirements evolve by time due to dynamic distribution of vehicles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI-C Evaluation on drone swarm sensing ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> illustrates a selected map of 1600 × 1600 meters in the city with the simulation time of 8 hours, where multiple drones navigate and perform traffic monitoring. Over 16 time periods, each corresponding a maximum flight time of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30min</annotation></semantics></math>, drones take off from one of the uniformly distributed base stations, travel to several cells to perform sensing (i.e., hover and observe vehicles), and return to a base station for recharging. In this scenario, a series of drone navigation and sensing over a flight time is modeled as a plan. The drone plans are generated using route planning strategies based on a power consumption model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, where each plan represents a path and its corresponding cost reflects the energy consumption of drone traveling over the path. To calculate energy consumption precisely, this paper chooses DJI Phantom 4 Pro model<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
            https://www.dji.com/uk/phantom-4-pro/info</span></span></span>. The parameters of the drone, including the weight, battery and field of view, the one used in earlier work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, this paper employs the <em id="S6.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouping plan constraints</em> strategy that divides drone plans based on semantic information (i.e., their spatial flying directions), denoted as <em id="S6.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-s</em>, rather than discomfort cost. Since each plan represents a navigation and sensing operation of a drone, plans can be grouped by their intended spatial range. This strategy is significant in a large spatio-temporal sensing missions, where drones anticipating future traffic surges can proactively navigate to those areas, recharge at nearby base stations, and continue sensing in the next period, even if those areas are currently sparsely populated with vehicles. Such foresight-driven action enhances long-term vehicle detection performance. This paper also sets a baseline method <em id="S6.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-d</em> that groups the plans based on their discomfort cost.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18088v1/UAVscenario.png" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/229;" width="429" height="229" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>The central business district of Munich, Germany with <math id="S6.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="144" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>144</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">144</annotation></semantics></math> square cells, <math id="S6.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> charging stations, and approximately <math id="S6.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,000</annotation></semantics></math> vehicles passing by per hour. Each drone has navigation and sensing plans under four flight directions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18088v1/DroneBasic.svg" id="S6.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/168;" width="215" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F9.sf1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F9.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The cost comparison.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.18088v1/DroneSensing.png" id="S6.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:229/212;" width="229" height="212" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F9.sf2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F9.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The drone sensing comparison.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 9: </span>Cost comparison of all methods in the drone swarm sensing scenario (<math id="S6.F9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> agents, <math id="S6.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> plans per agent and <math id="S6.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> time periods, i.e., <math id="S6.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8h</annotation></semantics></math> at total).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S6.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ VI-C Evaluation on drone swarm sensing ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows the optimal cost of <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-s</em> that defines <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> spatial groups based on directions: north, east, south, and west. This approach achieves relatively low mean discomfort cost, consuming less energy (<math id="S6.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2</annotation></semantics></math>kJ) compared to <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS</em>. Meanwhile, it efficiently utilizes energy resources for vehicle observation, resulting in the minimum inefficiency cost among all methods. Fig. <a href="#S6.F9.sf2" title="In Fig. 9 ‣ VI-C Evaluation on drone swarm sensing ‣ VI Performance Evaluation ‣ Strategic Coordination for Evolving Multi-agent Systems:A Hierarchical Reinforcement and Collective Learning Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9(b)</span></a> shows that the number of vehicles observed by drones matches up the real number of vehicles (task target) in the map of <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\times 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\times 12</annotation></semantics></math> grid cells. It shows that <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-s</em> misses <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32.53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32.53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32.53\%</annotation></semantics></math> vehicles and senses <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.78\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.78</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.78\%</annotation></semantics></math> extra vehicle. The effectiveness of <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-s</em> is attributed to the ability of drones utilizing MARL to anticipate traffic surges and proactively navigate to those areas. Even if the short-term benefits are not evident, MARL-based methods excel in long-term vehicle detection efficiency and accuracy, outperforming <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS-P</em> in the inefficiency cost, which has <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.13\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60.13</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.13\%</annotation></semantics></math> missed and <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26.64\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>26.64</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26.64\%</annotation></semantics></math> extra sensed vehicles. <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-s</em> addresses this challenge by grouping plans by flying direction, effectively narrowing the drone flight ranges to traffic-dense areas and enhancing training efficiency. As a consequence, its combined cost is <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.47\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.47</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.47\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower than <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em>. In contrast, <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-d</em> assists drones to choose the route with lower energy consumption, but sacrifice the efficient vehicle observation (i.e., high inefficiency cost), resulting in a combined cost that is <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.27\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.27</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.27\%</annotation></semantics></math> higher than <em id="S6.SS3.p4.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-s</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Discussion and New Insights</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental results demonstrate the superior performance of the proposed approach <em id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em>. Several scientific insights on <em id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> are listed as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em><span id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> achieves a win-win synthesis of long- and short-term optimization performance improvement in multi-agent systems.</span>
Unlike DCL approaches such as <em id="S7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS</em>, <em id="S7.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> leverages deep neutral networks to effectively adapt and tackle complex tasks, leading to <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.69\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23.69</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.69\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower system costs despite higher task complexity. Compared to traditional MARL methods such as <em id="S7.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MAPPO</em>, <em id="S7.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> enables agents to efficiently observe and aggregate the plans of others via a tree communication structure, minimizing the system-wide cost by <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31.29\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>31.29</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31.29\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em><span id="S7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> combines the strengths of both high-level strategies to improve scalability and Pareto optimality.</span>
The <em id="S7.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouping plan constraints</em> strategy in <em id="S7.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> effectively reduces computational complexity by limiting the number of actions each agent must consider, ensuring scalable learning, faster training convergence and improved cost efficiency, even as the number of plans and agents increases. Meanwhile, the <em id="S7.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouping behavior ranges</em> strategy allows each agent to autonomously adjust its behavior within optimized ranges, significantly lowering both discomfort and inefficiency costs. This strategy is particularly effective with fewer plans and agents. By integrating these two complementary strategies, <em id="S7.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> achieves a balance between scalability and solution quality, leading to more Pareto-efficient outcomes across diverse multi-agent scenarios. Moreover, it can switch to a single strategy in special cases, e.g., using <em id="S7.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-P</em> for high complexity of target tasks and <em id="S7.p3.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL-B</em> for low plan volume.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em><span id="S7.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> ensures the individual privacy-preserving.</span>
Leveraging <em id="S7.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">EPOS</em>, <em id="S7.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> allows agents to self-determine their plan options without leaking their private information, e.g., the plan generation function and individual constraints. Furthermore, the proposed approaches facilitates fully decentralized information exchange, ensuring that agents do not share sensitive data with a central entity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Each agent makes decisions locally, disclosing only minimal necessary aggregated information for coordination. Note that the critic network in <em id="S7.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> is centralized but not privacy-intrusive without accessing to agents’ private information. This privacy-preservation is essential when the approach involves heterogeneous agents from diverse entities, including private users and companies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em><span id="S7.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> provides a generic and versatile solution for real-world applications.</span>
The extensive evaluations in smart city applications illustrate that <em id="S7.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> minimizes energy consumption, ensures load-balancing and improves sensing performance. Unlike approaches constrained to specific scenarios or simplified evaluation benchmarks such as multiple particle environments, <em id="S7.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> generalizes across diverse environments by allowing agents to abstract complex conditions into structured plans without requiring modifications to the core learning algorithm. Moreover, <em id="S7.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouping plan constraints</em> supports flexible grouping strategies, such as discomfort cost and semantic infor of the plan. This modular design ensures <em id="S7.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> can be seamlessly applied to a wide range of real-world tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion and Future Work</span></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, the proposed <em id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> for decentralized combinatorial optimization problem in evolving multi-agent systems is feasible by minimizing both individual agent costs and overall system-wide costs. Its hierarchical framework effectively integrates the long-term decision-making of MARL with the decentralized coordination efficiency of DCL. This synthesis allows <em id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HRCL</em> to outperform standalone MARL and DCL approaches in terms of (1) discomfort and inefficiency cost minimization, and (2) low computation and communication overhead. The introduction of two high-level strategies for grouping plan constraints and behavior ranges further improves Pareto-optimal outcomes across varying scales of agent populations and plan complexities. Experimental evaluation based on real-world data from two state-of-the-art pilot projects, i.e., energy self-management and drone swarm sensing, provide a proof-of-concept for the broader applicability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, the designed approach can be further improved towards several research avenues: (1) Explore fully decentralized training paradigms where agents learn and adapt their policies without relying on centralized coordination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. (2) Study more types of <em id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grouping plan constraints</em> strategy to adapt to complex real scenarios, e.g., grouping plans that occur during similar time windows (peak or off-peak usage in energy demands). (3) Use blockchain, differential privacy and holomorphic encryption to further ensure secure information exchange, strengthening trust among agents without leaking private information.</p>
</div>
</section>
